# This file is generated by dune, edit dune-project instead
opam-version: "2.0"
synopsis: "Native desktop apps with a web UI, through webview bindings"
description: """
The package provides two libraries.

owebview is the binding to the webview library
(https://github.com/webview/webview): create a native desktop window
rendered by the system web engine, navigate to a URL or to inline HTML,
and call back and forth between OCaml and JavaScript. It covers the full webview
0.12 C API and stays deliberately low-level.

owebview.desktop adds what a desktop application needs around that window,
and which webview itself does not provide: native menu bars and modal
dialogs, the application icon, and locating the web assets next to the
executable."""
maintainer: ["frederic.ln.lang@gmail.com"]
authors: ["Frédéric Lang"]
license: "MIT"
tags: ["webview" "gui" "desktop" "webkit" "html" "javascript" "bindings"]
homepage: "https://github.com/korkorran/Owebview"
doc: "https://korkorran.github.io/Owebview"
bug-reports: "https://github.com/korkorran/Owebview/issues"
build: [
  ["dune" "subst"] {dev}
  [
    "dune"
    "build"
    "-p"
    name
    "-j"
    jobs
    "@install"
    "@runtest" {with-test}
    "@doc" {with-doc}
  ]
]
dev-repo: "git+https://github.com/korkorran/Owebview.git"
# NOTE: this block overrides the `depends` generated from (depends ...) in
# dune-project — dune lets the template win and does not emit the field twice.
# It lives here because dune cannot express opam filters such as
# `{os != "macos"}`, and conf-gtk3-webkit is only needed where the backend is
# GTK/WebKitGTK: not on macOS (WebKit/Cocoa) nor on Windows (WebView2).
# Keep it in sync with (depends ...) in dune-project.
depends: [
  "dune" {>= "3.0"}
  "ocaml" {>= "4.08"}
  "dune-configurator" {>= "3.0" & build}
  "conf-c++"
  "conf-gtk3-webkit" {os != "macos" & os != "win32"}
  "odoc" {with-doc}
]
x-maintenance-intent: ["(latest)"]
